d("hobby", "I just got into birdwatching. What do I need?", "Not much: a pair of binoculars (8x42 is a good all-round choice), a field guide or an app like Merlin Bird ID for your region, and a notebook. Go early in the morning near trees and water, move slowly and listen as much as you look.", sys=S1)
d("hobby", "I want to try painting but I'm bad at drawing", "Painting doesn't require strong drawing first. Start with acrylics or watercolours and simple subjects: skies, landscapes with big shapes, or abstract colour studies. Follow a beginner video, paint often and don't judge the early pieces.", sys=S2)
d("hobby", "best way to start running a vegetable garden in a small yard", "Start with 2 or 3 raised beds, about 1.2 m wide. Pick easy crops: lettuce, radishes, beans, tomatoes and herbs. Choose a spot with 6+ hours of sun, use good compost-rich soil and water regularly in the morning.")
d("hobby", "I'd like to get into photography on my phone", "Learn light first: shoot during the golden hour and avoid harsh midday sun. Clean the lens, tap to focus and adjust exposure, use gridlines for composition and try the portrait or night modes. Edit lightly in a free app like Snapseed.")
d("hobby", "how do I start playing poker without losing money", "Learn the hand rankings and basic strategy first, play free or play-money games, and when you play for real, set a small fixed budget you can lose. Avoid chasing losses, and treat it as entertainment.", sys=S3)
d("hobby", "good board games for 2 players?", "Try Patchwork, Jaipur, 7 Wonders Duel, Codenames Duet (cooperative) or Carcassonne. For something shorter, Love Letter takes about 15 minutes.")
d("hobby", "I want to start woodworking with no tools", "Begin with a few basics: a tape measure, a square, a handsaw, a drill/driver, clamps and sandpaper. Start with simple projects like a shelf or a planter box, use pine or poplar, and always wear eye and ear protection. A workshop class is a good way to learn safe habits.")
d("hobby", "do I need to know music theory to write songs", "No. Plenty of songwriters write by ear. A little theory helps, though: knowing a few common chord progressions (I-V-vi-IV, for example) and how keys work speeds you up and gives you options when you're stuck.")
d("hobby", "how to start collecting vinyl records", "Get a decent turntable and speakers first, since a bad stylus can damage records. Start with albums you love, check discs for scratches before buying and store them upright in sleeves away from heat. Local record shops are great for browsing.")
d("hobby", "Anyone can learn to draw?", "Largely yes. Drawing is a skill built through observation and practice. Start by drawing what you see, like simple objects, using basic shapes and learning to measure proportions. Short daily sketches matter more than rare long sessions.")
d("culture", "What's the difference between baroque and classical music?", "Baroque (about 1600 to 1750, like Bach and Vivaldi) is ornate, with complex counterpoint and a steady bass line. Classical (about 1750 to 1820, like Mozart and Haydn) has clearer, balanced melodies, simpler textures and structured forms such as sonata form.")
d("culture", "what is the Renaissance", "A period from roughly the 14th to the 17th century, beginning in Italy, when art, science and learning were revived from classical sources. It produced Leonardo da Vinci, Michelangelo and Galileo, and spread new ideas through the printing press.")
d("culture", "Explain the plot of Hamlet briefly", "The Danish prince Hamlet learns from his father's ghost that his uncle Claudius murdered the king and married Hamlet's mother. He pretends to be mad while deciding what to do, accidentally kills Polonius, and is sent to England. After more deaths, including Ophelia's, Hamlet duels Laertes, and nearly everyone dies, including Claudius and Hamlet himself.")
d("culture", "what is jazz", "A music genre that began in African American communities in New Orleans in the early 20th century. It's known for improvisation, swing rhythms and blue notes. It has evolved into many styles, including bebop, cool jazz and fusion.")
d("culture", "Is Shakespeare worth reading?", "Yes, though it's often easier to watch a performance first. His stories and characters still resonate, and the language becomes easier with an annotated edition. Start with 'Macbeth' or 'Much Ado About Nothing'.")
d("culture", "what's the difference between a museum and a gallery", "Museums collect, preserve and display objects, often with a focus on history, science or art. Galleries mainly exhibit art, often for sale. Public galleries may be like museums.")
d("culture", "Why is 'Citizen Kane' considered great?", "It was innovative for its time, with deep-focus cinematography, a nonlinear narrative told through multiple viewpoints, and bold use of sound and lighting. Its story of a powerful man's rise and loneliness is also thematically rich. Whether you enjoy it is a separate matter, and many viewers find it slow at first.")
d("culture", "who are the Beatles", "An English rock band formed in Liverpool in 1960, made up of John Lennon, Paul McCartney, George Harrison and Ringo Starr. They became hugely influential in the 1960s and changed pop music with songwriting, studio experimentation and albums like 'Revolver' and 'Sgt. Pepper'.")
d("culture", "explain what a haiku is", "A short Japanese poem, traditionally with three lines of 5, 7 and 5 syllables, often about nature and a moment of insight. In English, the syllable count is often relaxed.")
d("culture", "What is the plot of Romeo and Juliet in one sentence?", "Two teenagers from feuding families fall in love and die tragically because of a series of misunderstandings.")
d("practical", "How do I check if a website is trustworthy?", "Look for HTTPS, a clear About page and contact details, and check the domain name for odd spellings. See who owns it, search for independent reviews, and be careful with sites that pressure you to buy fast or ask for unusual payment methods.")
d("practical", "how do I clean a cast iron pan", "Wash it with hot water and a brush, using a little soap if needed (modern soap is fine), dry it immediately and rub on a thin layer of oil. Avoid soaking, and don't put it in a dishwasher.")
d("practical", "How do I measure my ring size at home?", "Wrap a thin strip of paper or string around the base of your finger, mark where it overlaps, and measure the length in millimetres. That's the circumference; look it up on a ring size chart. Measure at the end of the day, when fingers are widest.")
d("practical", "What should be in a basic first aid kit?", "Adhesive bandages in several sizes, sterile gauze and tape, antiseptic wipes, tweezers, scissors, disposable gloves, pain relievers, an elastic bandage, a thermometer and a card with emergency numbers. Check expiry dates twice a year.")
d("practical", "can i use vinegar on my hardwood floors", "Use it with caution. Undiluted vinegar can dull a finish over time. If you must, dilute it heavily and test in a hidden spot; for sealed floors, a cleaner made for hardwood is the safer choice.")
d("practical", "what's the best way to organise my files on my computer", "Use a small number of top-level folders (e.g. Work, Personal, Archive), name files with dates in YYYY-MM-DD format, avoid deeply nested folders and archive old material yearly. Consistency beats a perfect system.")
d("practical", "How do I get rid of fruit flies?", "Remove the source: ripe fruit, open bins and drains with residue. Make a trap with a bowl of apple cider vinegar plus a drop of dish soap, covered with cling film with small holes. Empty it every few days.")
d("practical", "tips for moving with a cat", "Keep her in a closed room with her bed and litter during the move, use a secure carrier, and set up one quiet room at the new place first with familiar items. Keep her indoors for a few weeks before letting her explore.")
d("practical", "How do I get started with meal prep?", "Pick 2 or 3 simple recipes, such as a grain, a protein and roasted veg, cook on one day for the week and store in containers. Cooked food usually lasts 3 to 4 days in the fridge; freeze extra portions.")
d("practical", "how do I stop my glasses sliding down my nose", "Have an optician tighten the frames and adjust the nose pads and temple tips. Silicone grips or anti-slip nose pads can help a lot. If the glasses are old, the frames may have stretched.")
d("practical", "Is it better to hand wash or use a dishwasher?", "A modern dishwasher generally uses less water and energy than hand washing and cleans well when you scrape rather than pre-rinse and run full loads. Hand washing makes sense for delicate items or small loads.")
d("practical", "what's the best way to carry a heavy backpack", "Use both shoulder straps, tighten them so the pack sits close to your back, use the hip belt to move weight to your hips, and put the heaviest items close to your spine and in the middle of the pack.")
d("sports_chat", "what are the rules of offside in football", "A player is offside if, when the ball is played to them, they are nearer to the opponent's goal line than both the ball and the second-last defender (the goalkeeper usually counts as one), and they are in the opponent's half. Being offside is only penalised if the player becomes involved in play. You can't be offside from a throw-in, goal kick or corner.")
d("sports_chat", "how does scoring work in tennis", "Points go 0 (love), 15, 30, 40, then game. At 40-40 it's deuce, and a player must win two points in a row. A set is won at six games with a two-game lead, and a match is best of three or five sets.")
d("sports_chat", "What does a marathon training plan look like?", "Typically 16 to 20 weeks with three to five runs a week: short easy runs, one mid-week workout, and a weekly long run that builds from 8 to about 32 km, followed by a taper in the last two weeks. Increase mileage gradually, around 10% a week, and include rest.")
d("sports_chat", "Why do athletes warm up?", "A warm-up raises muscle temperature and heart rate, increases blood flow and prepares the nervous system, which can improve performance and may reduce injury risk. Dynamic movements such as leg swings and light jogging are better than long static stretching before activity.")
d("sports_chat", "how long is an NBA game", "Four 12-minute quarters of game time, so 48 minutes of play. With stoppages, timeouts and halftime, a game usually lasts about two and a half hours.")
d("sports_chat", "I want to start lifting weights. Where do I begin?", "Start with 2 or 3 full-body sessions a week using basic lifts: squat, hinge (deadlift or Romanian deadlift), push, pull and carry. Learn form with light weights, and add weight gradually. A coach or a beginner programme such as a simple linear progression helps.")
d("sports_chat", "Cricket vs baseball, what's the difference?", "Both involve a bat and ball, but cricket uses a flat bat, a wicket target and an oval field with bowlers delivering the ball after a bounce, and a batter may hit in any direction. Baseball has a diamond-shaped fan field, a round bat, a pitcher throwing from a mound, and foul territory.")
d("nature_chat", "why do leaves change colour in autumn", "Chlorophyll, the green pigment, breaks down as days shorten and temperatures fall. This reveals yellow and orange pigments (carotenoids) that were there all along, while some trees also make new red pigments (anthocyanins).")
d("nature_chat", "How do bees make honey?", "Bees collect nectar, store it in their honey stomach and pass it to hive bees, which add enzymes and spread it in the comb. They fan it with their wings to evaporate water until it thickens, then seal the cells with wax.")
d("nature_chat", "why do birds migrate", "To find better food and breeding conditions. In winter, insects and plants are scarce in the north, so many birds fly south, then return in spring when food is plentiful. They navigate with the sun, stars, Earth's magnetic field and landmarks.")
d("nature_chat", "are sharks dangerous to humans", "A few species can be, but attacks are rare, with only a handful of fatal incidents worldwide each year. Many more sharks are killed by humans than the reverse. Avoid swimming at dusk or dawn, near fishing activity, or in murky water.")
d("nature_chat", "How do trees communicate?", "Some forest trees share resources and chemical signals through underground fungal networks and release airborne chemicals when damaged by insects. How much this is cooperation versus incidental exchange is debated among scientists.")
d("nature_chat", "what is a tsunami", "A series of large ocean waves caused by sudden displacement of water, usually from an undersea earthquake, landslide or volcanic eruption. In open water they are barely noticeable, but they grow tall as they reach shallow coastlines.")
d("nature_chat", "do plants feel pain", "Plants respond to damage, light, touch and chemicals, but they have no nervous system or brain, so there is no evidence they feel pain as animals do. Their responses are chemical and hormonal.")
d("nature_chat", "why is the desert cold at night", "Dry air has little water vapour to trap heat, and desert surfaces radiate heat quickly after sunset. With clear skies and little cloud cover, temperatures can drop sharply even after a hot day.")
d("time_chat", "How do I manage my time with ADHD?", "External structure helps: visual calendars, timers, body doubling (working alongside someone), breaking tasks into very small steps, and rewarding yourself for starting. Try reducing choices and making important tasks visible. A clinician can discuss medication and tailored strategies.")
d("time_chat", "What's the Pomodoro technique?", "You work in focused 25-minute blocks, called pomodoros, separated by 5-minute breaks, and take a longer break of 15 to 30 minutes after four blocks. It helps start tasks and sustain focus.")
d("time_chat", "How do I prioritise when everything is urgent?", "List everything, then ask what has a real deadline, what is blocking others and what has the biggest consequence if delayed. Some things labelled urgent are only important to the person asking. Say what you'll do first and ask others to confirm or reprioritise.")
d("time_chat", "How do I stop procrastinating on big projects?", "Break the project into small tasks, schedule the first one for a specific time, and make it easy to start (open the document, write one rough paragraph). Aim for progress rather than perfection, and use a deadline with a friend for accountability.")
d("time_chat", "best way to use a to-do list", "Keep it short: choose 3 key tasks for the day, write tasks as clear actions (\"call dentist\", not \"dentist\"), and review it each morning. Move things you keep avoiding into smaller steps, or delete them.")
d("time_chat", "is multitasking really bad?", "For tasks that need attention, such as writing or analysis, switching between them has a cost in time and errors. Combining an automatic task with a thinking one, like walking and listening, is fine.")
d("quick_ask", "synonym for 'important'", "Significant, crucial, essential, vital, key, critical.")
